### Diretórios com ponto: NEGA TUDO, permite o que precisa existir ###
# Ruling do dono (2026-07-29): "não seria mais fácil ignorar todos os caminhos de diretório
# iniciados por '.'? Manter só os que realmente precisam existir? Assim evita dos agentes vomitarem
# sem querer e evita seu retrabalho de limpeza sem os barrar."
#
# Inverte o problema. Antes: perseguir cada nome de rascunho novo que um agente ou um script
# inventa — e perder sempre, porque a lista só cresce depois do estrago. Medido no dia em que a
# regra foi escrita: os scripts já criavam `.seed-releases/`, `.regressor-full/` e `.seed-debut/`,
# e NENHUM dos três estava coberto (`.seed/` casa só com `.seed` exacto). Três buracos abertos que
# ninguém tinha visto.
#
# Só dois diretórios com ponto são rastreados nesta árvore (medido: `git ls-files`), e ambos têm de
# passar — `.github/` carrega a CI inteira. Um terceiro que venha a precisar de existir acrescenta
# a sua linha aqui, deliberadamente, e é essa deliberação que é o ponto.
#
# Nota de semântica: `.*/` só casa DIRETÓRIOS (barra final), a qualquer profundidade. Ficheiros com
# ponto na raiz (`.gitattributes`, `.gitignore`, `.DS_Store`, `.ci-phase-clock`) não são tocados, e
# `.claude/worktrees/` continua a precisar da sua regra própria porque `worktrees` não começa por
# ponto. A negação tem de ser do DIRETÓRIO em si — o git não reinclui um ficheiro cujo diretório-pai
# está excluído.
.*/
!.claude/
!.github/
!.crumbs/

### Teko build artifacts ###
# (0.3.0.30 taxonomy) `.tkb` is now EXCLUSIVELY the Teko Binary codec artifact (docs/design/
# tkr-regression-format.md §0) — always a build product, always ignored. The authored regression
# specs are `.tkr` (Gherkin, tracked by default, no rule needed).
*.tkb
*.tkcov

*.wat
*.mjs
### Teko project build outputs (regenerated by `teko build`; never committed) ###
examples/**/*.c
examples/regressions/match_pattern_bindings/match_pattern_bindings
examples/regressions/optionals/optionals
examples/regressions/noncapturing_lambda_lift/noncapturing_lambda_lift

# native-backend build artifacts (teko build .)
/teko.c
/teko
/bin/
# native-build output dirs anywhere (teko build . -> <proj>/bin)
bin/
# gen2/paranoid fixpoint scratch build dirs (never committed — they perturb byte-identity)
bin2/
binp/
# (0.3.0.30 R2) the regression runner builds each curated regressor with `-o bin` relative to the
# regressor dir — keep those working trees clean so they never perturb the fixpoint / byte-identity
# checks. (Redundant with the global `bin/` above; named for the crumb.)
examples/regressions/**/bin/
# self-host verification output dirs (the gen1/gen2/gen3 byte-identity gate)
/gen1/
/gen2/
/gen3/
# dot-prefixed variants of the same gate dirs (.gen2/.gen3 — PR #100 committed them by accident)
.gen*/
# the fixpoint gate's own scratch root — the SAME accident as `.gen*/` above, with a name that
# `.gen*/` does not cover. PR #95 committed .fixpoint/gen2{,.c} and gen3{,.c}: 28 MB of generated
# artefacts, two of them 10 MB C files headed "generated by teko — do not edit". Generated C is
# OUTPUT and is never tracked; the one tracked C from this machinery is `bootstrap/teko.c`, and
# only because it is a deliberately harvested seed. No green was ever faked by this — the gate
# wipes its scratch root before it builds (scripts/fixpoint_gate.sh, `rm -rf "$W"`).
.fixpoint/
cobertura.xml

# the native-dry-gate's recorded BASE signature (scripts/native_dry_gate.sh --save). The script
# instructs every agent to write one, and the `.*/` rule above covers only dot-DIRECTORIES, so
# without this line the next `git add -A` commits a local measurement as if it were source — the
# same accident that put .fixpoint/ and .gen*/ in the tree.
.native-base.sig

# per-worktree downloaded teko compiler (scripts/fetch_teko.sh)
.teko/
# per-worktree PROVISIONED seed (scripts/ci_provision_teko.sh) + downloaded archives — NEVER commit a binary
.seed/
teko-*.tar.gz
teko-*.zip
gd-*/
/out/
# Ad-hoc gate output directories: `build_with_seed_fallback.sh [OUT_DIR]` and
# `fixpoint_gate.sh <gen1> <proj> <WORK_DIR>` take the path from the caller, so an agent running the
# ritual by hand names dirs like `out-gen1/`, `out-mp/`, `fx-work/`. They hold gen1/gen2/gen3, the
# emitted teko.c and the .tsym — OUTPUT, never source (the one tracked C is bootstrap/teko.c).
/out-*/
/fx-work*/
# throwaway gen1 self-host build dir (teko build . -o gen1out)
/gen1out/
/.work/
/build/
/bin/

# native-build output dir (teko build -> <proj>/target for some layouts) — NEVER commit
target/

# Transients left by a producer run executed IN-TREE (an agent measuring locally, or a hand-run
# of scripts/produce_assets.sh). In CI they live in a throwaway runner and nobody sees them; on a
# developer's or an agent's checkout they land next to the source and get swept into a `git add
# -A`, which is exactly how one of them nearly rode into a memory-file commit.
.teko-seed-fallback-wt/
.ci-phase-clock
.seed/

# The verdict-channel unit test's scratch file. It writes a WORKING-DIRECTORY-relative name
# (a POSIX-absolute /tmp path is a claim about the host, and Windows has none) and removes it
# again; this entry covers the run that aborts between the two.
.teko-verdict-emit-test.chan*
.teko-verdict-path-test.chan*
gd-*/
asset-log-*.txt

# build artefacts of the local trampoline (cc(bootstrap/teko.c): the wagon-15 compiler)
.rung-c/

# scratch of the regression pool runner tests (regression_test.tkt: run_pool_*/pool_*)
.regr-pool-*/
# scratch of the regression BATCH runner (.regr-batch-test/) — a sibling of the pool scratch above
# and the SAME accident as `.fixpoint/`, with a name the `.regr-pool-*/` rule does not cover. Nine
# of these rode into cargo/0.3.1-estreitamento-fluxo before anyone noticed; tiny, but scratch is
# never tracked.
.regr-batch-test/
.probe/

# examples/regressions/<x>/out/ — a regressor build DIRECTORY. `ref_mutable_binder` rode into the
# tree with its 107 KB linux-x86_64 binary and its .tsym committed; a built artifact of one host is
# never tracked, and the harness rebuilds every regressor anyway.
examples/regressions/*/out/

# examples/regressions/<x>/bin_*/ — the two-route measurement directories (bin_c / bin_nat). Same
# rule as `out/`: a built artifact of one host is never tracked. Four of them rode in on one
# `git add -A` during this very degrau.
examples/regressions/*/bin_*/

# Diretórios de saída de gerações intermédias (gen2/gen3, sondas de medição).
# Acrescentado depois de um censo apanhar `out2/{teko,teko.c,teko.tsym}` no índice:
# saída NUNCA é fonte, e o `out/` sozinho não cobria os irmãos numerados.
out*/

